[THOUGHT]  
The patient presents with a one-month history of binocular diplopia (double vision) that worsens with sustained visual effort (e.g., computer use) and improves with rest — a classic fatigable pattern. Though the patient has not formally tested for uniocular vs. binocular diplopia, the symptom’s improvement with eye closure or rest strongly suggests it is *binocular* (i.e., resolves when one eye is covered), as true monocular diplopia does not improve with rest or eye closure. The absence of reported ptosis does not rule out myasthenia gravis (MG), as ocular MG can present with isolated diplopia early on, and ptosis may be subtle or absent initially. Critically, the acetylcholine receptor antibody (AChR Ab) binding assay is positive and elevated (value: 9 — above reference threshold, typically >0.4 nmol/L or >0.5 nmol/L depending on lab; “Present (elevated)” confirms seropositivity). This, combined with the fatigable, effort-dependent diplopia, is highly consistent with **ocular myasthenia gravis**.

[SUMMARY]  
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Ocular Myasthenia Gravis (Seropositive)**  
  - Supported by:  
    • Fatigable, effort-dependent binocular diplopia (worsens with prolonged near work, improves with rest);  
    • Positive and elevated acetylcholine receptor antibody (AChR Ab) binding assay (result: “Present (elevated)”, value 9);  
    • Absence of confirmed ptosis does not exclude diagnosis — isolated diplopia is a common initial presentation of ocular MG.  

**Next Steps Recommended:**  
- Formal assessment of ocular motility and alignment (e.g., cover-uncover test, prism adaptation) to confirm binocularity and identify affected muscles;  
- Consider ice pack test or rest test for objective documentation of fatigability;  
- Neurology referral for confirmatory evaluation (e.g., repetitive nerve stimulation, single-fiber EMG if serology equivocal);  
- Initiation of symptomatic therapy (e.g., pyridostigmine) and discussion of immunomodulatory options if symptoms impair function;  
- Chest imaging (CT or MRI) to evaluate for thymoma — indicated in all AChR Ab–positive patients regardless of ocular vs. generalized presentation.

